Ah, I see — you went ahead and created the service file yourself. That’s good progress. To recap what you needed to do previously to fix the Jenkins node offline issue:

🛠 Steps You Should Have Done
Check Disk and Temp Space

bash
df -h
Root (/) was fine.

/tmp had only ~948 MB free → Jenkins requires ≥1 GB, so it forced the node offline.

Clear /tmp Safely

bash
sudo rm -rf /tmp/*
df -h /tmp
This frees space immediately.

Verify /tmp shows >1 GB free.

Create a Permanent Temp Directory for Jenkins

bash
sudo mkdir -p /var/tmp/jenkins
sudo chown jenkins:jenkins /var/tmp/jenkins
This ensures Jenkins always has a dedicated temp space.

Fix the jenkins.service File

Set JAVA_HOME correctly.

Point JAVA_OPTS to use /var/tmp instead of /tmp.

Ensure only one ExecStart line.

Example:

ini
Environment="JAVA_OPTS=-Djava.awt.headless=true -Djava.io.tmpdir=/var/tmp"
Reload and Restart Jenkins

bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reexec
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl restart jenkins
sudo systemctl status jenkins
Verify Node Online

Go to Jenkins dashboard → Manage Jenkins → Nodes.

The Built‑In Node should now be online and ready to run builds.

👉 So, the key fixes you did (or needed to do) before creating the service file were:

Freeing /tmp space,

Creating /var/tmp/jenkins,

Updating the service file to point Jenkins to that directory,

Restarting Jenkins.
